<p>Danes, along with voters in Greenland and the Faroe Islands, are casting ballots Tuesday to elect all 179 seats in the Folketing. Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen is seeking a third term after calling an early parliamentary election. Several outlets say her standing improved after she pushed back against President Donald Trump’s threats to annex Greenland. Even so, the race remained competitive, with voters also focused on domestic issues such as living costs, taxes, and public policy disputes. The campaign also underscored a broader shift in Danish politics. Several reports say support for the traditional far right has weakened, but tougher immigration policies have moved into the mainstream under Frederiksen’s Social Democrats. That shift has drawn criticism from some left-leaning voters and Muslim Danes, who say exclusionary rhetoric has become more normalized. At the same time, Frederiksen’s support for Ukraine and her defense of Greenland have raised her international profile, leaving Denmark with an election shaped by unusually visible foreign-policy tensions as well as unresolved domestic divisions.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/0OPX7NikBWQlt4IBRps9uvIf3aTqPQJWubF84sMSyM84UUIlEIxnpjY1k7YPWcXzGqwI889lRvnoti6j1NDA4eJW20HCBr7Qpy0KprNkim4M-ApWjkqEfNCeBN8kot86tOPyDNb9qZDFT7soWAvgmYkvOiqqva3o22Mwk5Cr1BE86vuJQjZ9N2xcUxp5UJ2DjZzmWHgZysYp3Hl-g0eeZEGhSaBQDWsFR58629mAiUBcwm_czwVikYKK7PRf91mIep7tx0wyYeJO31zURQ' alt='People pass an election advertisement for Mette Frederiksen, Denmark&#x27;s Prime Minister and leader of the Social Democrats party, in Copenhagen on Monday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Undecided voters: An Epinion poll published Monday found that nearly one in five Danish voters still had not decided how to vote, underscoring the race’s late uncertainty.</li><li>Coalition strains: Denmark’s coalition government was fraying, and two other party leaders were challenging Frederiksen, helping turn the vote into a close contest.</li><li>Holiday issue: Parties also debated whether to restore the Great Prayer Day holiday, which Frederiksen’s government abolished in 2024. The dispute showed how cultural and economic questions mixed with security concerns during the campaign.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mette Frederiksen and the Social Democrats: Frederiksen campaigned on a mix of economic redistribution and a steady line on security and foreign affairs, including a revived wealth tax that she presented as part of her case for another term. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/denmark-how-to-watch-election-pro/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Troels Lund Poulsen and Venstre: Poulsen opposed Frederiksen’s proposed wealth tax, arguing that it could push Denmark’s richest residents to leave the country and hurt national competitiveness. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/denmark-how-to-watch-election-pro/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Mayasa Mandia: The 23-year-old voter said she would vote for the left, but not for the Social Democrats, because she believes far-right commentary has become normalized under Frederiksen’s government, including discussions about banning prayers at her university. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/24/how-the-danish-mainstream-embraced-far-right-immigration-politics'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Financial Times: The newspaper argued that fragmentation on Denmark’s right could shape the outcome as much as Frederiksen’s own popularity, potentially making it easier for her to stay in power. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/a7175780-6024-4455-b457-c49c764d58f4'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/a7175780-6024-4455-b457-c49c764d58f4'>Danish premier Frederiksen poised to retain power as right splinters</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/24/how-the-danish-mainstream-embraced-far-right-immigration-politics'>Denmark election: far right has slowed under Frederiksen – but at what cost?</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/denmark-how-to-watch-election-pro/'>How to watch Denmark’s elections like a pro</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/24/mette-frederiksen-heads-into-danish-elections-as-a-global-figure-but-a-divisive-one-domestically_6751750_4.html'>Mette Frederiksen heads into Danish elections as a global figure, but a divisive one domestically</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-denmark-election-mette-frederiksen-trump-greenland/'>Danish PM seeking re-election in the wake of Greenland dispute with Trump</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/danes-vote-in-an-election-clouded-by-trumps-greenland-desires'>Danes vote in an election clouded by Trump&#x27;s Greenland desires</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://apnews.com/article/denmark-parliament-election-greenland-c6951fa94fc601efa1401318112a3a04'>Danes, Faroe Islanders, and Greenlanders vote to elect 179 seats of the Folketing. Danish prime minister Mette Frederiksen seeks a third term in office during the aftermath of the Greenland crisis. (AP)</a> - apnews.com</li></ul>